  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:04 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "David H. Thompson (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. THOMPSON: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: The Elections Clause requires state legislatures specifically to perform the federal function of prescribing regulations for federal elections. States lack the authority to restrict the legislatures' substantive discretion when performing this federal function."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0000-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. THOMPSON: Yes, Your Honor. Thank you. So the Court is reviewing the decision -- there was an order on February 4th of the North Carolina Supreme Court, and it was accompanied 10 days later by a written opinion, and in that written opinion, there was a liability determination that the Elections Clause did not apply, and, importantly, there was also a remedial determination -- and we can see this at Petition Appendix 142 -- where it empowered the North Carolina Supreme Court, empowered the lower state court to draw the maps if necessary."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Counsel, this case is from a state supreme court that interpreted and applied a state constitution. So it would be -- help -- be helpful if you would take some time to explain what we're -- what exactly we are reviewing, what decision we're reviewing, and what is the basis of our jurisdiction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "David H. Thompson (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0000-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. THOMPSON: Well, Your Honor, our -- our position on the merits is to take as given state law as interpreted by the North Carolina Supreme Court. We're not asking this Court to second-guess or reassess. We say take the North Carolina Supreme Court's decision on face value and as fairly reflecting North Carolina law, and when one does that, we see that there's a violation of the Elections Clause, and -- and that's why we're here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: -- you don't dispute that there could be judicial review by the state court of a federal constitutional violation?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What do you think is the best case supporting this substance/procedure distinction?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Thompson",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I -- I -- I would say Palm Beach County. I think the Florida Supreme Court --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0002-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "vacated the opinion of the Florida Supreme Court, and it sent it back on remand for the Florida Supreme Court to assess and to clarify whether it was, in fact, using the state constitution to operate as a substantive limit."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Thompson",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "if the North Carolina Constitution had said partisan gerrymandering is -- cannot be allowed if there's an efficiency ratio of more than 7 percent, then that would be a judicially discoverable and manageable standard."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Absent the Election Clause, are -- is this term so unmanageable that you're saying that the North Carolina court would not have power to determine what Free Election Clause meant in their constitution?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "what sorts of concerns might --- might the Founders have had if state constitutions were allowed to trump over state legislatures?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In interpreting the state statutes, can a state court rely on canons of interpretation that say interpret those state statutes in light of state constitutional provisions?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0002-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What about the historical practice over time, which has certainly developed in a way that state constitutions do regulate federal elections? What weight, if any, do we place on that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0002-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And on the theory that at that point the state court would not be acting as a court but would be acting more as a legislature?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Thompson",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0002-0009",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 63,
        "start": 63
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the Court's decision from 1946, Murphree, where it is talking about the Rules Enabling Act and is setting up the line between substance and procedure."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0003-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 71,
        "start": 71
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF NEAL K. KATYAL ON BEHALF OF THE PRIVATE RESPONDENTS"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Neal K. Katyal (private respondents)",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 72,
        "start": 71
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "For 233 years, states have not read the Elections Clause the way you just heard. There are two reasons to affirm. One is that when enacting legislation, there's no such thing as an independent state legislature. The other is that North Carolina statutes authorized what the North Carolina court did. I'll focus on the first. Petitioners' idea that state legislatures created by state constitutions are independent of them is wrong. It is rejected by the Articles of Confederation, rejected by the early state constitutions, rejected by the founding practice, especially New York, where judges vetoed federal election bills. It's also rejected by this Court in cases such as Smiley and Hildebrant."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0004-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 86,
        "start": 86
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: What about the -- what about the approach set out by Justice -- by former Chief Justice Rehnquist? Does that --is that also a Lollapalooza? Does that have a --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Katyal",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0004-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 87,
        "start": 87
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KATYAL: So, for 233 years, this Court has never second-guessed a state court interpretation of its own constitution in any context."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0005-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 108,
        "start": 108
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: And -- and whatever the exact wording of the standard is that you think applies on the constitutional side, would that be implicated in this case?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0005-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 112,
        "start": 112
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: So you would defend that as -- as consistent with an appropriate understanding of the Elections Clause?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "State Respondents",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0006-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 129,
        "start": 129
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First, Petitioners' argument cannot be reconciled with Smiley. Smiley held that because the Elections Clause invokes the state legislature's law-making function, the conditions which attach to the making of state laws apply. Judicial review is such a condition,"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0006-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 131,
        "start": 131
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Mr. Verrilli, the -- how far would you go with that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0007-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 150,
        "start": 150
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Well, sure there was controversy, and where --and this isn't -- political gerrymandering is no new thing, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Verrilli",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0007-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 151,
        "start": 151
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. VERRILLI: No, I -- I doubt it, but those are means of implementing a fundamental principle. Those aren't fundamental principles themselves. And the fundamental principle that I -- that the North Carolina Supreme Court articulated as I read the opinion is that you don't want the electoral districts to manipulate it, be manipulated so that one group of voters is severely disadvantaged as compared to another group of voters of a different party of the same size."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0007-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 162,
        "start": 162
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the three-judge court on remand"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Elizabeth B. Prelogar",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0007-0003",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 168,
        "start": 168
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "would wreak havoc in the administration of elections across the nation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0008-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 171,
        "start": 171
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: General, I should have asked this question to Mr. Verrilli, so apologies to both of you. Just what is the status of the state court proceedings right now?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Prelogar",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0008-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 175,
        "start": 175
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "GENERAL PRELOGAR: I think this Court has rejected that distinction already in cases like Smiley and Hildebrant, and they rejected exactly the theory that my friend has proposed about looking at the federal function."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f7fd24dd6d83df19b5d7b43a27c92d4389a345f50d694e4aceb2b469cb92aa3c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f7fd24dd6d83-0008-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 181,
        "start": 181
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: May I ask you a couple of questions about your interpretation of two federal statutory provisions that you cite, 28 U.S.C. 2(a)(C) and 2(a)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1271",
      "confidence": 0.0,
